Apt repository hosted on GitHub Pages
=====================================

This document describes the GitHub-Pages-hosted apt repository for
the DuckDB packaging fork.  Two audiences are addressed:

  1. END USERS who want a one-line `apt install duckdb`.
  2. MAINTAINERS who run debian/scripts/publish-apt-pages.sh.

For end users, only section 1 is relevant.

------------------------------------------------------------
1. End users — installing DuckDB via apt
------------------------------------------------------------

The repository is served at:

    https://mgajda.github.io/duckdb-deb/

Supported suites (use the codename of YOUR distribution):

    Debian trixie  -> SUITE=trixie
    Debian sid     -> SUITE=sid
    Ubuntu 22.04   -> SUITE=jammy
    Ubuntu 24.04   -> SUITE=noble

Three-step install (replace SUITE with your codename above):

    # 1. Trust the maintainer's archive key
    sudo install -d -m 0755 /etc/apt/keyrings
    curl -fsSL https://mgajda.github.io/duckdb-deb/duckdb-deb-archive-key.asc \
        | sudo gpg --dearmor -o /etc/apt/keyrings/duckdb-deb.gpg

    # 2. Add the apt source
    echo "deb [signed-by=/etc/apt/keyrings/duckdb-deb.gpg] \
          https://mgajda.github.io/duckdb-deb SUITE main" \
        | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/duckdb-deb.list

    # 3. Update + install
    sudo apt-get update
    sudo apt-get install duckdb libduckdb-dev

This is a *third-party* apt repository.  It is not affiliated with
Debian, Ubuntu, or the upstream DuckDB project.

Verifying the maintainer key (optional but recommended)
-------------------------------------------------------

Before trusting the archive key, you may verify its fingerprint
matches the maintainer's published identity:

    gpg --show-keys < <(curl -fsSL \
        https://mgajda.github.io/duckdb-deb/duckdb-deb-archive-key.asc)

The displayed fingerprint should match the one listed at
github.com/mgajda/duckdb-deb under SECURITY.md once that file
exists.  (TODO: ship SECURITY.md.)

Removing the apt source
-----------------------

    sudo rm /etc/apt/sources.list.d/duckdb-deb.list \
            /etc/apt/keyrings/duckdb-deb.gpg
    sudo apt-get update

------------------------------------------------------------
2. Maintainers — publishing a release to the apt repo
------------------------------------------------------------

The publish flow is laptop-local; the GPG signing key never
leaves the maintainer's machine.  CI does NOT run this script.

Prerequisites (one-time)
------------------------

1. Generate a signing key (or import an existing one):

    gpg --quick-generate-key \
        'duckdb-deb-signing <mjgajda@gmail.com>' \
        ed25519 sign 5y

   Note the fingerprint.  Save it somewhere durable (password
   manager, paper); you will need it again when the key expires
   in 5 years and you rotate.

2. Make it gpg's default key (recommended):

    echo "default-key <FPR>" >> ~/.gnupg/gpg.conf

   OR plan to pass `SIGNING_KEY=<FPR>` to the publish script
   every invocation.

3. Enable GitHub Pages on the fork:

    Settings -> Pages -> Source: "Deploy from a branch"
                        Branch: "gh-pages" / "/ (root)"
                        (gh-pages does not exist yet; the publish
                        script creates it on first run.)

4. Install the tooling:

    sudo apt-get install reprepro gh

   gh must be authenticated:

    gh auth login

Publishing a tag
----------------

After CI's release job has attached the matrix .debs to a GitHub
Release (which requires a `git push fork v<X.Y.Z>-deb<N>`):

    debian/scripts/publish-apt-pages.sh v<X.Y.Z>-deb<N>

The script:
  - downloads the release's .deb + .dsc files
  - clones (or creates) the gh-pages branch
  - copies the reprepro config + your public key into it
  - includes each .deb in every published suite (over-inclusive
    until task #26 lands; see the script comments)
  - signs the dists/<suite>/Release files with your gpg key
    (you will be prompted to unlock)
  - commits and pushes gh-pages

Typical runtime: ~2 minutes plus gpg-unlock delay.

Verification
------------

After ~5 minutes (GH Pages cache propagation), curl one of the
Release files to confirm:

    curl -fsSL https://mgajda.github.io/duckdb-deb/dists/trixie/InRelease

This should print a signed plain-text manifest.

Spin up a fresh Debian container and exercise the full path:

    podman run --rm -it docker.io/library/debian:trixie bash
    # ... follow the end-user steps above ...

Rolling back
------------

Each publish-apt-pages.sh run commits a new state of gh-pages.
To roll back, force-push the previous commit:

    cd ~/.cache/duckdb-apt-pages/gh-pages
    git log --oneline | head      # find the previous good commit
    git push fork HEAD~1:gh-pages --force

Key rotation
------------

When the signing key expires (or is compromised), rotate:

  1. Generate a new key with the same UID.
  2. Re-run publish-apt-pages.sh with the new key as default.
     The script writes the new public key into
     duckdb-deb-archive-key.asc, replacing the old one.
  3. Announce the rotation in the GH release notes for the next
     tag, including the old AND new fingerprints, so existing
     users can verify the transition.

Users will see "NO_PUBKEY <FPR>" from apt-get update until they
re-import the key via the install steps above.

------------------------------------------------------------
3. Internals (for future maintainers)
------------------------------------------------------------

Why reprepro and not aptly?
---------------------------

Both produce static apt repos.  Differences:

  reprepro:
    - In Debian/Ubuntu repos since the early 2000s
    - Config is one file (debian/reprepro/conf/distributions)
    - No state outside the repo dir (no daemon, no database)
    - Stable, simple, less powerful

  aptly:
    - Newer (Go, 2014+)
    - Models the repo as a snapshot DB; "publish snapshot" promotes
    - Supports multi-suite atomic updates
    - Daemon mode + REST API for managed pipelines

We use reprepro because the mental model fits "publish a tag"
better -- include this .deb, regenerate Release, sign, push.  No
need for the snapshot/publish layer aptly adds.

Other options surveyed:

  Cloudsmith, Packagecloud:   commercial, third-party storage
  Launchpad PPA:              free, but Launchpad rebuilds from
                              source (slow turnaround), Ubuntu-only
                              upload path, separate-account setup
  Self-hosted (own server):   ongoing operational cost
  Sigstore/cosign:            apt does not natively verify sigstore

GH Pages won on: cost (free), simplicity (no extra service to
manage), and maintainer control (signing key stays local).

Repo layout
-----------

After the first publish, the gh-pages branch contains:

    dists/
      trixie/
        InRelease       (Release + inline signature)
        Release
        Release.gpg     (detached signature)
        main/
          binary-amd64/Packages.{gz,xz}
          binary-arm64/Packages.{gz,xz}
          source/Sources.{gz,xz}
      sid/...           (same shape)
      jammy/...
      noble/...
    pool/
      main/d/duckdb/
        libduckdb1_<ver>_amd64.deb
        libduckdb1_<ver>_arm64.deb
        libduckdb-dev_<ver>_amd64.deb
        ...
    conf/
      distributions     (copy of debian/reprepro/conf/distributions)
    duckdb-deb-archive-key.asc
    index.html
    .gitignore          (excludes reprepro's db/, lists/, incoming/)

The dists/<codename>/ trees are what apt reads; pool/ is the
file store.  Users see whichever codename matches their distro.
